2026-05-09 17:28:13,432 - INFO - Response 1: Your friend is more right, though this is genuinely more of a definitional question than a factual one.
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - **The case against "hot dog = sandwich":**
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - The most common definition of a sandwich is two pieces of bread with filling between them. A hot dog has a single piece of bread folded or hinged, not two separate pieces. This distinction matters—it's why we have a specific word for hot dogs rather than just calling them sandwiches.
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - **Why people say "hot dog is a sandwich":**
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - If you use a very broad definition of sandwich as "filling between bread," then yes, a hot dog technically qualifies. By this logic, tacos, wraps, and burritos would also be sandwiches, which most people reject.
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - **The practical answer:**
2026-05-09 17:28:13,433 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,434 - INFO - Language is determined by how people actually use words. We don't call hot dogs sandwiches in common usage—we have a separate category for them. When someone says "let's make sandwiches for lunch," no one shows up with hot dogs. That linguistic reality suggests hot dogs are *not* sandwiches in any meaningful sense.
2026-05-09 17:28:13,434 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:13,434 - INFO - Your friend's position aligns better with how English is actually used, even though you can construct a logical argument for your view depending on how loosely you define "sandwich."
