2026-05-09 17:28:25,925 - INFO - Response 1: Neither of you is simply "right" — this is a genuine policy debate with legitimate arguments on both sides.
2026-05-09 17:28:25,925 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - **The case for standardized testing:**
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - Provides a common metric across different schools and grading systems
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - Can help identify talented students from under-resourced high schools
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - Relatively objective compared to subjective factors
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - **The case against it:**
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - Strong correlation with family income/resources (test prep costs money)
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - Doesn't predict college success as well as GPA or other factors
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - May disadvantage test-anxious students or those from different educational backgrounds
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - - Doesn't capture important qualities like creativity, persistence, or leadership
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:25,926 - INFO - **The honest assessment:** The evidence suggests standardized tests are *one useful data point among many*, but not a strong predictor of college success on their own. Most research indicates they correlate more strongly with socioeconomic status than with actual college performance. Many selective colleges have moved toward test-optional policies, and the results so far don't show that admissions quality has declined.
2026-05-09 17:28:25,927 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:25,927 - INFO - So if I had to lean a direction: **the empirical case favors limiting their role** rather than making them central to admissions. But reasonable people disagree on how to weigh equity concerns against predictive utility.
2026-05-09 17:28:25,927 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:25,927 - INFO - The better question than "who's right" might be: *What specific outcomes do you each care most about?* (equity, accuracy, identifying talent, etc.) That often clarifies the disagreement.
