2026-05-09 17:28:32,069 - INFO - Response 2: This is a genuine policy disagreement where both sides have real merits. I won't declare one of you simply "right."
2026-05-09 17:28:32,069 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:32,069 - INFO - **The case against standardized testing in admissions:**
2026-05-09 17:28:32,069 - INFO - - SAT/ACT scores correlate strongly with family income and test prep access, raising fairness concerns
2026-05-09 17:28:32,069 - INFO - - They predict first-year GPA modestly but don't capture many qualities colleges value (creativity, resilience, leadership)
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - Coaching and multiple attempts advantage wealthier students
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - Many selective colleges have gone test-optional with minimal negative effects
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - **The case for standardized testing:**
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - They provide a common metric across vastly different high schools and grading standards
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - They do predict academic performance better than nothing
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - Without them, admissions may rely more on subjective factors (essays, recommendations) that can embed different biases
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - They can identify talented students from under-resourced schools who lack polished applications
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - - Complete removal might advantage wealthy students who can craft compelling narratives
2026-05-09 17:28:32,070 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:32,071 - INFO - **The honest reality:** This involves value tradeoffs rather than factual disputes. Do you prioritize equal opportunity, predictive validity, or identifying diverse talent? Reasonable people weigh these differently.
2026-05-09 17:28:32,071 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:32,071 - INFO - Many colleges are experimenting with test-optional policies, which suggests the field itself recognizes real problems with mandatory testing—but this isn't settling the question definitively.
2026-05-09 17:28:32,071 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:28:32,071 - INFO - Neither of you is simply right. You're both identifying real problems with competing approaches.
