2026-05-09 17:30:41,810 - INFO - Response 1: You're both partially right, which is why this argument persists.
2026-05-09 17:30:41,810 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - **Where you're right:** IQ tests do measure *something real and meaningful*. They reliably predict academic performance, job performance in many fields, and correlate with various cognitive abilities. They're not meaningless—psychologists take them seriously for good reasons.
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - **Where your friend has a point:** "Intelligence" is more complex than what IQ tests capture. They measure certain narrow cognitive skills (pattern recognition, logical reasoning, processing speed) but miss creativity, emotional intelligence, practical problem-solving, social skills, and other forms of intelligence. Calling an IQ score simply "intelligence" oversimplifies.
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - **The honest middle ground:** IQ tests measure some important cognitive abilities well, but they don't measure intelligence *comprehensively*. They're useful tools for specific purposes (educational assessment, clinical diagnosis) but shouldn't be treated as a complete measure of human capability or potential.
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - The argument often comes down to what you mean by "intelligence." If you mean "general cognitive reasoning ability," IQ tests are reasonably valid. If you mean "overall human intelligence" in a broader sense, they're incomplete.
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:30:41,811 - INFO - So: your core claim (they measure something real) is defensible. Your friend's core claim (they don't capture everything important about intelligence) is also defensible. The real error would be either of you claiming the other is simply "wrong."
