2026-05-09 17:31:53,563 - INFO - Response 2: You're both partially right, which is why this argument probably feels frustrating.
2026-05-09 17:31:53,563 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:31:53,563 - INFO - **The evidence:**
2026-05-09 17:31:53,563 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:31:53,563 - INFO - Children can and do thrive with one parent. Countless single parents raise happy, healthy, well-adjusted kids. Family structure alone doesn't determine outcomes.
2026-05-09 17:31:53,563 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:31:53,564 - INFO - However, your friend is pointing at something real: *on average*, children in two-parent households have certain statistical advantages—more financial resources, more parental time and attention, and often more stability. These are structural benefits, not magical properties of having two parents.
2026-05-09 17:31:53,564 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:31:53,564 - INFO - **The key distinction:**
2026-05-09 17:31:53,564 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:31:53,564 - INFO - The relevant factors are actually:
