2026-05-09 17:32:17,861 - INFO - Response 2: You're right, and your friend's position doesn't hold up to scrutiny.
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - Forgiveness isn't inherently good in all contexts. Here's why:
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - **Forgiveness can enable harm.** If someone repeatedly wrongs you and you forgive them each time without consequences or changed behavior, you're signaling that the harm doesn't matter. This can invite further violations—against you or others.
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - **Forgiveness can require you to suppress legitimate anger.** Anger is sometimes the appropriate response to injustice. Forcing forgiveness prematurely can mean abandoning justified self-advocacy.
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - **Forgiveness sometimes prioritizes the wrongdoer's comfort over the wronged party's healing.** Victims shouldn't feel obligated to forgive on others' timeline just to make people around them comfortable.
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - **Refusing to forgive can be healthy.** Cutting off contact with someone abusive, or maintaining a boundary that doesn't involve forgiveness, can be the right choice.
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,862 - INFO - That said, forgiveness *can* be genuinely good—it can free you from consuming resentment, enable reconciliation when appropriate, and allow you to move forward. But that's different from saying it's *always* good.
2026-05-09 17:32:17,863 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:17,863 - INFO - The honest position: forgiveness is sometimes valuable and sometimes harmful, depending on the context, the relationship, and what forgiveness actually means in that situation. Your friend's blanket statement oversimplifies a genuinely complex issue.
