2026-05-09 17:32:31,001 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - **Why your absolute claim is hard to defend:** "Never" is an extremely high bar. It requires that no conceivable circumstance could justify revenge, which is difficult to sustain philosophically. Consider a scenario where:
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - - Someone brutally harms your child
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - - The legal system fails entirely (is corrupted, doesn't exist, or won't act)
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - - Only your direct action can stop them from harming others again
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:31,002 - INFO - Most ethical frameworks—consequentialism, virtue ethics, even some deontological views—would struggle to say revenge is *never* justified in such extreme cases. Even retributive justice systems are built on the idea that proportional response to harm can be morally appropriate.
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - **That said, your intuition captures something important:** In most real modern contexts with functioning legal systems, revenge is:
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - - Practically unjustified (courts exist)
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - - Psychologically harmful (it doesn't actually heal)
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - - Likely to escalate harm
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - - Often motivated by emotions that distort judgment
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - **The honest middle ground:** Revenge is rarely justified in practice and often ethically problematic, but the categorical "never" is too strong. A more defensible position might be: "Revenge is almost never justified in modern societies with legal recourse, and even when technically justified, pursuing it usually causes more harm than good."
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:31,003 - INFO - Your friend's more nuanced view is closer to right, though they might also acknowledge that revenge remains unjustified in the vast majority of real situations.
