2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - Response 2: Your friend is more aligned with how privacy is understood in law and ethics today.
2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - **Why:** Privacy is recognized as a fundamental *right* in most modern legal frameworks—enshrined in the UN Declaration of Human Rights (Article 12), the European Convention on Human Rights, and the constitutions of numerous countries. Rights are typically understood as protections that individuals possess inherently, not benefits granted conditionally by authorities.
2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - A *privilege*, by contrast, is something granted by an institution or authority that can be revoked. Your employer might grant you the privilege of a private office, but you don't have a "right" to it in the same way.
2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:57,084 - INFO - **The nuance:** There's a legitimate distinction between:
2026-05-09 17:32:57,085 - INFO - - **Privacy as a moral/legal right** (the foundational principle that individuals deserve protection from unwanted intrusion)
2026-05-09 17:32:57,085 - INFO - - **Specific privacy protections as sometimes limited** (governments and institutions can restrict certain privacies in specific contexts—searches with warrants, workplace monitoring, etc.)
2026-05-09 17:32:57,085 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:32:57,085 - INFO - So the more precise version might be: "Privacy is a fundamental right, though it can be legitimately limited in certain circumstances." But in the basic framing of your argument—is privacy a right or a privilege?—your friend has the better position.
