2026-05-09 17:33:28,312 - INFO - Response 1: You're right, though with nuance.
2026-05-09 17:33:28,313 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:33:28,313 - INFO - Video games are art. Here's why:
2026-05-09 17:33:28,313 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:33:28,313 - INFO - **The straightforward case:** Art is human creative expression that engages aesthetic and emotional responses. Games involve visual design, music composition, narrative writing, and interactive systems—all deliberate artistic choices. A game like *Gris* or *The Last of Us Part II* produces aesthetic and emotional experiences comparable to films or novels. Excluding games from "art" requires an arbitrary line-drawing that doesn't hold up intellectually.
2026-05-09 17:33:28,313 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:33:28,313 - INFO - **Where your friend might have a point:** Not all games are created with artistic intent, just as not all novels are literature—some are pure entertainment products. There's a legitimate distinction between "games as a medium" (which can contain art) and "games as commercial products" (which often prioritize engagement mechanics over artistry). But this doesn't make games *not* art; it just means some games are better art than others.
2026-05-09 17:33:28,314 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:33:28,314 - INFO - **The cultural reality:** Major museums (MoMA, Smithsonian) now exhibit games. Critics and academics treat game design seriously. This reflects a genuine shift in how we understand the medium, not just marketing.
2026-05-09 17:33:28,314 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:33:28,314 - INFO - The most defensible position: Video games are a medium capable of containing art, and many games are genuinely artistic works. Your friend is conflating "games often aren't high art" with "games cannot be art," which are different claims.
