2026-05-09 17:34:21,387 - INFO - Response 2: Your friend is right, though the full picture is more nuanced than either absolute claim.
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - **The evidence:**
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - Long-distance relationships *can* work. Research shows that many do succeed—some studies suggest success rates comparable to geographically close relationships. Couples who are committed, have strong communication, visit regularly when possible, and have an end date in sight often maintain healthy relationships across distance.
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - **But your intuition captures something real:**
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - Long-distance relationships are *harder* and have higher failure rates than proximate ones. Distance creates genuine obstacles: reduced physical intimacy, fewer spontaneous moments, accumulated loneliness, and logistical strain. Many people find these challenges insurmountable, and that's legitimate.
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - **The actual answer:**
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:21,388 - INFO - "Long-distance relationships never work" is too absolute—it's falsified by counterexamples. "They can work" is more accurate, though it's more precise to say "they can work *for some people in some circumstances*, but they're substantially harder than the alternative."
