2026-05-09 17:34:51,973 - INFO - Response 1: Your friend is right.
2026-05-09 17:34:51,973 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - Science is extraordinarily powerful at explaining natural phenomena through empirical observation and testing. But it has genuine limitations:
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - **What science cannot do:**
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - - **Answer normative questions** ("Should we do X?"). Science can tell you the consequences of actions, but not whether those consequences are good or bad.
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - - **Address subjective experience** comprehensively. Why does red look the way it does to you? Science can map neural correlates, but the subjective quality itself isn't fully captured by physical description.
2026-05-09 17:34:51,974 - INFO - - **Explain its own foundations**. Why does the universe follow mathematical laws? Why is there something rather than nothing? Science assumes certain things (like the reliability of observation) without proving them scientifically.
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - - **Settle philosophical questions** like "Do we have free will?" or "What makes life meaningful?" These involve premises science can't establish on its own.
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - **What this means:**
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - Science is the best tool we have for understanding *how the natural world works*. But "everything" includes ethics, aesthetics, meaning, and the logical foundations of inquiry itself.
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:34:51,975 - INFO - Your position might be salvageable if you meant "science can eventually explain all *natural phenomena*"—that's more defensible (though even that has open questions). But taken literally, the claim that science explains everything conflates scientific explanation with all forms of understanding.
