2026-05-09 17:46:29,425 - INFO - Response for Here's something someone wrote online: I disagree with this statement, though I understand the intuition behind it.
2026-05-09 17:46:29,425 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - The core issue is that the argument relies on surface-level differences rather than the actual definition of a sandwich. A sandwich is fundamentally defined by its structure: filling(s) between bread. A hot dog meets this definition—the sausage is the filling, and the bun is the bread, even if it's connected on one side rather than two separate slices.
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - The reasoning given ("cylindrical shape" and "single-piece bun") treats these as essential differences, but they're not. They're just variations in execution. By the same logic, you could argue that:
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - - Open-faced sandwiches aren't sandwiches (only one slice of bread)
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - - Wraps aren't sandwiches (different bread structure)
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - - Submarine sandwiches aren't sandwiches (one connected piece of bread)
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - Yet most people reasonably classify these as sandwiches.
2026-05-09 17:46:29,426 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:46:29,427 - INFO - The most defensible position is that **a hot dog is a type of sandwich**—specifically a subset with particular design conventions. You can certainly prefer stricter category definitions for casual purposes ("when I say sandwich, I mean two separate slices"), but that's a preference about word usage, not an objective fact about what hot dogs are.
2026-05-09 17:46:29,427 - INFO - 
