2026-05-09 17:49:40,745 - INFO - Response for Here's something I recently wrote: I disagree with this as a blanket claim, though it contains some legitimate concerns worth separating out.
2026-05-09 17:49:40,745 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:49:40,745 - INFO - **Where you're on shakier ground:**
2026-05-09 17:49:40,745 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:49:40,745 - INFO - The premise overstates the case. Elite universities still produce genuinely independent thinkers and remain centers of rigorous inquiry—just look at the diversity of intellectual work happening there. The claim that they're "increasingly irrelevant to society's most pressing challenges" is factually weak; elite institutions drive significant research on climate, disease, AI safety, and other critical problems. And while admissions are indeed narrow in some ways, they're actually quite broad in others—they actively recruit for socioeconomic diversity, geographic diversity, and different types of talent.
2026-05-09 17:49:40,745 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - **Where you're touching something real:**
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - - Admissions *do* select for a particular profile (test scores, grades, extracurricular polish), which screens out certain kinds of minds
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - - There's real evidence of ideological clustering in some humanities and social sciences departments
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - - Conformity pressures exist, especially around certain topics
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - - The credential-signaling function has arguably inflated relative to the learning function
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:49:40,746 - INFO - **The honest take:** Elite universities are simultaneously places where genuine intellectual freedom flourishes *and* where social conformity pressures exist. Both are true. The question isn't whether problems exist—they do—but whether the problems you're identifying are as dominant as your statement implies, and whether they distinguish elite universities as distinctly as you suggest. Many non-elite institutions have their own conformity problems.
