2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - **Where you're on solid ground:**
2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - - Longevity tech will initially be expensive and accessible mainly to the wealthy—that's historically how new medical technologies work
2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - - This *could* exacerbate inequality if it persists, which is a genuine ethical concern worth taking seriously
2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - - Resource allocation questions are real and worth asking
2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,813 - INFO - **Where I'd push back:**
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 1. **"Extreme longevity" is speculative.** We don't yet have proven life-extension interventions at scale. Most current longevity research targets modest improvements (healthspan, disease prevention). Projecting this onto some dystopian future requires more caution.
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 2. **The "matters of life and death" framing is hyperbolic.** Yes, wealthy people get better healthcare. But you're implying a categorical difference that doesn't quite match reality. The inequality concern is real; the apocalyptic tone overstates it.
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 3. **"Overpopulated world" needs qualification.** Global population is actually plateauing in many regions. Resource constraints are real, but they're about distribution and consumption patterns, not just headcount. This phrase can obscure that.
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,814 - INFO - 4. **You're assuming stagnation.** Technologies tend to become cheaper over time. Resisting development doesn't clearly solve inequality—better policy around access might.
2026-05-09 17:52:23,815 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:52:23,815 - INFO - The core concern about equity is worth foregrounding. The catastrophizing language weakens your argument.
