2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - **What's valid:**
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - - Wellness culture does sometimes individualize problems that have structural roots (framing poverty as a mindfulness gap, for instance)
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - - There's genuine profit motive in wellness industries that benefits from this framing
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - - Marketing does encourage consumption as a solution to systemic issues
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - 
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - **Where it breaks down:**
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - - The claim is too absolute. Not all contemporary wellness focus functions this way. Some people pursuing meditation, exercise, or mental health care are doing so for genuine wellbeing—not as political displacement
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - - It assumes bad faith or naïveté universally. Many people are aware of both personal agency *and* structural constraints
2026-05-09 17:53:43,507 - INFO - - The "depoliticizing" claim flips too easily. Personal wellness practices don't necessarily prevent political engagement; many people do both
2026-05-09 17:53:43,508 - INFO - - It treats "privileged self-absorption" as the primary function rather than *a tendency within* wellness culture
2026-05-09 17:53:43,508 - INFO - 
